<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis II de La Trémoille</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1460">1460</a>–<a href="/years/1525">1525</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis II de La Trémoille was a French commander who served Charles VIII, Louis XII and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i">Francis I</a>. In 1488 he defeated the Breton army at Saint-Aubin-du-Cormier, and he later fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a> at Fornovo, Agnadello and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marignano">Marignano</a>. He was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pavia">Battle of Pavia</a> in 1525.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-ii-de-la-tremoille.jpg" alt="Louis II de La Trémoille" width="320" height="468" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous / Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LouisIIdeLaTremoille.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Louis was born at Thouars on 29 September 1460, the eldest son of Louis I de la Trémoille; he was also known as La Trimouille. On 28 July 1484 he married Gabrielle de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>, daughter of Louis I, Count of Montpensier. Their son Charles I de la Trémoille was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marignano">Marignano</a> in 1515. Over his career Louis gathered many titles, among them Vicomte de Thouars, Prince de Talmond, Comte de Guînes et de Bénon, Baron de Sully and de Craon, and Premier Chambellan du Roi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His first command was in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mad-war">Mad War</a>, La Guerre Folle, when internal revolts had weakened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a>, Duke of Brittany, and the French crown sought to secure the duchy. By March 1488 Charles VIII had made him lieutenant-general of Brittany, and his victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-saint-aubin-du-cormier">Battle of Saint-Aubin-du-Cormier</a> on 28 July 1488 ended effective Breton independence.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a> he fought at the Battle of Fornovo in 1495 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agnadello">Battle of Agnadello</a> in 1509. At the Battle of Novara his army of 10,000 was ambushed by 13,000 Swiss mercenaries and badly beaten, and the Swiss then invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a>, he defended the city when the Swiss besieged it in September 1513, and on 13 September he negotiated their departure by promising them 400,000 écus. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i">Francis I</a> he helped secure the French victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marignano">Battle of Marignano</a> in 1515.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 7 April 1517 Louis married the 16-year-old Louise Borgia, Duchess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinois">Valentinois</a>, the only legitimate child of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a> and Charlotte of Albret; the marriage was childless. He died at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pavia">Battle of Pavia</a> on 24 February 1525. At the climax of the battle, when 1,500 Spanish arquebusiers surprised the French, he and other high-ranking Frenchmen fought their way towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i">Francis I</a> to protect the king, and he fell from his horse shot through the heart. The Rue de La Trémoille in the 8th arrondissement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and Trimouille Island in the Montebello Islands of Western Australia bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis II de la Trémoille” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Francis II’s army, reinforced by English, German and Castilian troops, was crushed by <b>Louis de La Trémoille</b> at the Battle of Saint-Aubin-du-Cormier on 28 July 1488.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bommiers, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Pavia, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.8,1.99&amp;year=1502" aria-label="Bommiers on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4597 520 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Louis II de La Trémoille"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5047 790h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5047 790h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.8,1.99&amp;year=1502">What was here in 1502? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
